elements.description:
The component shall,
provide storage encryption with hardware security mechanisms to ensure the confidentiality and integrity of data at rest;
adhere to industry-standard cryptographic algorithms and provide key management functionalities that adhere to best practices for secure storage of encryption keys as outlined in the "Cryptographic algorithms and keylength in products";
The implementation must ensure that encryption keys are stored securely within dedicated hardware modules only, such as Trusted Platform Modules (TPM) or Hardware Security Modules (HSM), to prevent unauthorized access and tampering.
Generally, the implementation of cryptography must follow the guideline on "Cryptographic algorithms and keylength in products";

No external standard defines this measure.

Components shall,
provide authentication with username and password as an authentication method for all human user accounts;

enforce password complexity rules (if provided);
allow for password change for all human user accounts;
Components may,
provide the component owner the possibility to define password complexity rules for human users; including a minimum password length;

Components shall,
provide authentication with certificates as an authentication method for all human user accounts;

provide the capability to add and remove certificates to individual human user accounts;
authenticate human users only with certificates signed by Certificate Authorities that are configured as trusted;
support integration into enrollment, renewal, and revocation of certificates processes of a pki (if applicable);
validate certificates upon each authentication attempt;
determine valid certificates by checking the certificate's validity period, revocation status, and the authenticity of the issuing CA;
store certificates securely, adhering to the crypto policy;

Component shall
* provide an immutable source of trust leveraging hardware-based security and;
* enforce authentication of
   - mutable code by the immutable source on every boot;
   - the bootloader;
* use either a public key or a hash of a public key;
* support deactivation of public keys whose private key has been leaked or misused and activation
of a spare public key

Component should
* provision sufficient space for keys;
* support renewal of secure boot keys.

The component shall
* have the capability to establish a secure channel to safeguard the integrity, authenticity and the confidentiality of transmitted data;
* authenticate itself to the communication partner.

The component shall
* have the capability to provide a secure channel to safeguard the integrity and the confidentiality of data received;
* accept data only from authenticated communication partners.

Components shall
* provide an authentication mechanism based on a password for all machine-to-machine access;
* allow for password change used for the machine-to-machine access by accordingly privileged users;

unless excluded by a compulsory state of the art specification.

No external standard defines this measure.

Components shall
* provide authentication with certificates as an authentication method for all machine user accounts;
* provide the capability to add and remove certificates to individual machine user accounts;
* authenticate machine users only with certificates signed by Certificate Authorities that are configured as trusted;
* support integration into enrollment, renewal, and revocation of certificates processes of a pki (if applicable);
* validate certificates upon each authentication attempt;
* determine valid certificates by checking the certificate's validity period, revocation status, and the authenticity of the issuing CA;
* store certificates securely, adhering to the crypto policy.

Components shall
* provide authentication with a password as an authentication method for all debug interfaces;
* allow for password change for the password associated with the debug interfaces;
* provide the user with a second factor to approve any access for all debug interfaces;
* The authentication method provided by the component may require an username;

No external standard defines this measure.

The component shall
* provide authentication based on public key as an authentication method for all debug interfaces;
* provide the capability to add and remove keys related to those debug interfaces;
* authenticate humans only with keys signed by certificate authorities that are configured as trusted;
* support integration into enrollment, renewal, and revocation of key processes of a pki;
* validate keys upon each authentication attempt;
* determine valid keys by checking at least the key's validity period, revocation status, and the authenticity of the issuing CA;
* store key pairs securely, adhering to the crypto policy;
* provide the user with a second factor to approve any access for all active debug interfaces.

No external standard defines this measure.

The component shall
* be made available only if a patent has been filed for the essential parts of the intellectual property contained in it or;
* be made available only under an appropriate license, or both;

It is a commercial measure and has no CRA Annex I mapping.

No external standard defines this measure.

The component shall
* support hardware-based isolation to run trusted and untrusted code in separate environments;
* have the capability to provide cryptographic proof that its execution environment is untampered;
* only include cryptographic modules within the TEE that are compliant with FIPS 140-2 or equivalent.

The TEE should be isolated from the main operating system, with access controlled by hardware-level security mechanisms.

The component shall
* actively monitor its physical diagnostic and test interfaces and report attempts to access them;
* detect physical tampering with the enclosure or components and notify a configurable set of recipients (e.g., log entry, alarm output or network message).

The component shall
* continuously monitor security mechanisms and security events using commonly accepted industry practices to detect, characterize and report security breaches;
* automatically notify a configurable set of recipients upon detection of an unauthorized change or integrity violation.
